WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:16.626
I crawled to the hatch and made my way back to the gallery.

2
00:00:16.627 --> 00:00:20.372
The storm was raging, and no one could stand upright.

3
00:00:20.373 --> 00:00:25.952
Finally at midnight Captain Nemo gave the orders to sink below the waves and escape the storm.

4
00:00:25.953 --> 00:00:33.460
We headed northeast, away from the shores of New York. We were also moving away from Canada, much to Ned's despair.

5
00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:38.023
Like Captain Nemo, he spent most of his time alone during these days.

6
00:00:38.024 --> 00:00:45.380
But Conseil and I stuck together, gazing out the gallery windows and tracing our route on the map as we crossed the Atlantic.

7
00:00:45.381 --> 00:00:53.261
By May 28 we were within 120 miles of Ireland. Ned came out of his cabin and peppered me with questions.

8
00:00:53.262 --> 00:00:55.856
Was the submarine headed for Great Britain?

9
00:00:55.857 --> 00:00:59.856
Did I think Captain Nemo would attempt to sail through the English Channel?

10
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:06.499
Was he taking us near France? I had no idea what to reply because the captain remained in his cabin.

11
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.499
We could only follow our course on the map.

12
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:17.158
During the day of May 31, the submarine traveled in circles; it seemed to be seeking a spot that it could not find.

13
00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:25.317
I was up on the platform at noon when Captain Nemo came to take the submarine's bearings—a task his lieutenant usually performed.

14
00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:29.587
He did not say a word to me, but he looked gloomier than ever.

15
00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:35.721
What could make him feel so unhappy? Was it because we were so close to European shores?

16
00:01:35.722 --> 00:01:41.991
Did he have some distressing memories of his abandoned country? Was he feeling remorse or regret?

17
00:01:41.992 --> 00:01:47.904
I was haunted by these questions and had a premonition that the captain's secrets would soon be revealed.

18
00:01:47.905 --> 00:01:53.642
The next day, June 1, the Nautilus continued to hunt for something in the water.

19
00:01:53.643 --> 00:01:57.634
Again it was Captain Nemo who came up to the platform at noon.

20
00:01:57.635 --> 00:02:06.634
The sea was beautiful and the sky was clear. Off to the east I could see a large steamship on the horizon about eight miles away.

21
00:02:06.635 --> 00:02:13.777
No flag fluttered from its mast, so I couldn't tell its country of origin. The captain took the submarine's bearings.

22
00:02:13.778 --> 00:02:17.777
"It is here," he said as he turned and went below.

23
00:02:17.778 --> 00:02:24.610
Had he seen the steamship, which was now changing course and seemed to be heading for us? I could not tell.

24
00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:28.277
I returned to the gallery and the Nautilus began to sink.

25
00:02:28.278 --> 00:02:34.602
A few minutes later, it rested on the sea bottom at a depth of 2,500 feet.

26
00:02:34.603 --> 00:02:38.920
I looked through one of the windows and saw nothing except quiet water.

27
00:02:38.921 --> 00:02:44.182
But through the other window I saw a large object encrusted with white shells.

28
00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:49.753
As I looked closer, I realized that it was a ship that must have sunk a long time ago.

29
00:02:49.754 --> 00:02:56.936
"At one time that ship was called the Marseillais, but it was renamed," said Captain Nemo, who had appeared at my side.

30
00:02:56.937 --> 00:03:05.546
"After a heroic battle, it sank with 356 sailors onboard rather than surrender to the British in 1794.

31
00:03:05.547 --> 00:03:08.674
Today is the anniversary of its sinking."

32
00:03:08.675 --> 00:03:15.571
My eyes widened as I recalled my French history. "You mean that's the Avenger?" I exclaimed.

33
00:03:15.572 --> 00:03:23.071
"Yes, the Avenger," said Captain Nemo, stretching out his hand toward the wreck. "A good name!"

34
00:03:23.072 --> 00:03:27.650
My eyes did not leave the captain, who was gazing reverently at the wreck.

35
00:03:27.651 --> 00:03:33.983
I might never know Captain Nemo's real name, where he was from, or where he was going.

36
00:03:33.984 --> 00:03:40.396
But I had just seen him moved by this shipwreck, and that gave me a clue to his character and his past.

37
00:03:40.397 --> 00:03:45.475
It was a strange hatred that had locked Captain Nemo inside the Nautilus with his crew.

38
00:03:45.476 --> 00:03:51.618
And it was a hatred that time could not diminish. I wondered if he still hungered for revenge.

39
00:03:51.619 --> 00:03:57.039
The Nautilus began to slowly rise to the surface, and the Avenger disappeared from view.

40
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:04.951
Soon a slight rolling motion told me that we were in the open air. At that moment I heard a dull boom.

41
00:04:04.952 --> 00:04:08.261
I looked at the captain, but he did not move.

42
00:04:08.262 --> 00:04:12.181
"Captain!" I said. "What was that sound?"

43
00:04:12.182 --> 00:04:18.927
He did not answer, so I left him and climbed up to the platform. Ned and Conseil were already there.

44
00:04:18.928 --> 00:04:22.014
"Where did that sound come from?" I asked.

45
00:04:22.015 --> 00:04:25.189
"It was cannon fire," replied Ned.

46
00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:35.419
I looked toward the east, where I had last seen that steamship. It was headed straight for us and putting on steam, but it was still miles away.

47
00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.744
"What is that ship, Ned?" I asked.

48
00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:48.816
"From its rigging and sails, it looks like a warship," said Ned. "May it reach us, and, if necessary, sink this cursed submarine."

49
00:04:48.817 --> 00:04:55.657
"Now, how will it do that?" asked Conseil. "It can't fire at us if we sink beneath the waves."

50
00:04:55.658 --> 00:04:58.975
I didn't give Ned a chance to respond.

51
00:04:58.976 --> 00:05:02.912
"Can you recognize which country it belongs to?" I asked.

52
00:05:02.913 --> 00:05:06.737
Ned squinted at the ship in the distance for a few moments.

53
00:05:06.738 --> 00:05:12.098
"No, Professor Aronnax. I can't tell where it's from because it's not flying any flag.

54
00:05:12.099 --> 00:05:17.054
But it's definitely a warship because there's a long pennant flying from its mainmast."

55
00:05:17.055 --> 00:05:21.785
For a quarter of an hour, we watched the ship rapidly steaming toward us.

56
00:05:21.786 --> 00:05:25.547
Thick black smoke was pouring from its two funnels.

57
00:05:25.548 --> 00:05:32.563
I had a hard time believing that anyone could see the Nautilus from that distance, but the ship did seem focused on us.

58
00:05:32.564 --> 00:05:46.293
"Professor," said Ned, "if that vessel passes within a mile of us, I'm going to throw myself into the sea, and I advise you two to do the same. It will pick us up and we'll be off this wretched submarine for good."

59
00:05:46.294 --> 00:05:51.452
I didn't reply to Ned's suggestion but continued to watch the ship approach.

60
00:05:51.453 --> 00:05:57.523
"Master, you may recall that we have some experience with swimming," said Conseil.

61
00:05:57.524 --> 00:05:59.912
"Yes, Conseil, I remember that—"

62
00:05:59.913 --> 00:06:04.603
I stopped speaking as a puff of white smoke burst from the bow of the ship.

63
00:06:04.604 --> 00:06:12.341
A few seconds later, something heavy made a big splash near the submarine's stern, and I heard a loud explosion.

64
00:06:12.342 --> 00:06:18.682
"What? They're firing at us!" I exclaimed. "Surely they can see that there are men onboard."

65
00:06:18.683 --> 00:06:22.817
"That may be exactly why they're firing on us." Ned looked at me.

66
00:06:22.818 --> 00:06:26.151
And suddenly I understood what was happening.

67
00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:32.492
I thought back to November when we were hunting the giant narwhal onboard the Abraham Lincoln.

68
00:06:32.493 --> 00:06:41.738
When Ned's harpoon hit the Nautilus, Captain Farragut must have realized that he was dealing with a dangerous submarine and not a sea creature.

69
00:06:41.739 --> 00:06:44.984
He obviously hadn't kept that news to himself.

70
00:06:44.985 --> 00:06:50.476
Ships had probably been crisscrossing the oceans in search of this underwater vessel ever since.

71
00:06:50.477 --> 00:06:56.881
To my horror, I also realized that Captain Nemo had used the Nautilus as an instrument of revenge.

72
00:06:56.882 --> 00:07:03.373
On the night when he'd locked us in that cell and drugged our food, the submarine must have attacked another ship.

73
00:07:03.374 --> 00:07:07.555
The man buried in the coral cemetery must have died in that battle.

74
00:07:07.556 --> 00:07:11.286
Someone knew who Captain Nemo really was.

75
00:07:11.287 --> 00:07:18.372
Someone knew that he was out to avenge some horrible wrong, and now whole nations were hunting him down!

76
00:07:18.373 --> 00:07:27.350
I faced the grim truth. Instead of meeting friends onboard this approaching ship, we could only expect merciless enemies.
